WEBVTT

NOTE Fake sample transcript for the demo. Tom Becker, City of Millbrook Parks Department.

00:00:01.000 --> 00:00:07.000
Ian: Thanks for making time, Tom. Can you walk me through how your team keeps the parks maintained during the growing season?

00:00:08.000 --> 00:00:24.000
Tom: Sure. We've got fourteen parks and about forty acres of what I'd call the difficult stuff. Drainage ditches, retention ponds, the slopes along the greenway. From May to September we're mowing pretty much nonstop.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.000
Ian: Which parts are hardest to keep up with?

00:00:29.000 --> 00:00:45.000
Tom: The ditches and the steep banks. You can't get a riding mower down there, so it's two guys with string trimmers for a whole day. Honestly, the slopes are where my budget goes to die.

00:00:46.000 --> 00:00:50.000
Ian: Tell me about the last time that didn't go the way you wanted.

00:00:51.000 --> 00:01:06.000
Tom: Last August a contractor rolled a mower on the bank by Fenwick Pond. Nobody got hurt, thank goodness, but we lost the machine and two weeks of schedule.

00:01:07.000 --> 00:01:10.000
Ian: What does mowing those areas cost you today?

00:01:11.000 --> 00:01:20.000
Tom: We spend about ninety thousand a year on contract mowing, and a big chunk of that is the hard-to-reach areas.

00:01:21.000 --> 00:01:25.000
Ian: Who else is affected by how this work gets done?

00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:40.000
Tom: Residents. If a crew starts at seven in the morning near the houses on Linden Street, I get three emails before nine. The noise complaints are constant.

00:01:41.000 --> 00:01:44.000
Ian: What have you tried to make it easier?

00:01:45.000 --> 00:02:04.000
Tom: We used to spray the ditch banks. Then a group of parents found out we were spraying near the playground and came to a council meeting. We stopped the next week. We're not going back to herbicide near the playgrounds.

00:02:05.000 --> 00:02:08.000
Ian: What other goals or rules shape how you handle this?

00:02:09.000 --> 00:02:22.000
Tom: Fire, honestly. The brush along the back of the greenway gets bone dry by July. The fire marshal flagged it twice last summer.

00:02:23.000 --> 00:02:26.000
Ian: How do decisions like this get made?

00:02:27.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Tom: I can approve anything under twenty-five thousand. Above that it goes to the council. If something cut what we spend on those slopes, I could get it approved for next season.
